---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/canberra-ink-landscape.avif";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="Tang Yin–inspired ink landscape of Canberra: eucalyptus trees beside Lake Burley Griffin, Parliament House across the water and Telstra Tower among misty hills."
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <p class="artwork-caption">
    <em>Canberra in ink</em> · A Tang Yin–inspired landscape.
    <a href={heroImage.src} target="_blank" rel="noopener">View the full artwork (new tab)</a>
  </p>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    Most of what survives from the Ming painter Tang Yin is not by Tang Yin.
    Workshops in his own city were forging his signature within his
    lifetime, and the copies never stopped: seals were carved to match,
    colophons were written in borrowed hands, and by the time modern museums
    started buying, nobody --- not the dealers, not the collectors, not
    always the museums --- could fully untangle the pile. This course teaches
    you to untangle it anyway. Each week hands you one more instrument ---
    paper and ink chemistry, brushwork, seals, colophons, the market that
    made forgery profitable, and finally what a trained statistical model
    can and can't add --- and you spend the semester turning those
    instruments on real, still-disputed paintings. You also paint your own study of a shared Tang Yin–inspired landscape,
    research a disputed historical painting, then create and defend a revised
    painting. Making the marks yourself helps you ask what visual resemblance
    can actually tell you about authorship. No prior painting experience is required.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    Built for graduate students in art history or a related humanities
    discipline who can already read a scholarly argument and want to build
    one of their own, evidence class by evidence class. No Chinese language
    background is assumed --- primary sources are supplied in translation ---
    but you should arrive comfortable with close visual reading and prepared
    to defend a specific, falsifiable claim rather than a general
    impression. If "I think it's probably genuine" is as far as your
    reasoning usually goes, week 2 is built to push past it.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>The weekly schedule and what happens in each teaching seminar.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>What is marked, what it is worth, and when it is due.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>Who teaches the course and how to reach them.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Policies" href="/policies/">
      <p>Late work, attendance, and what "declare your assistance" means here.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="The plates" href="/paintings/">
      <p>
        The six paintings this site is built from, and what each museum is
        willing to claim about who made them.
      </p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .artwork-caption {
    margin-block: -1rem 2rem;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
</style>
